"""Independent workflow guides, without affiliate creatives or coupon claims."""

GUIDES = [
    ('ai-meeting-notes', 'Choose an AI meeting note taker',
     'Choose meeting transcription around capture, review, export and privacy rather than a headline allowance.',
     '''<h2>Choose the workflow before the subscription</h2>
<p>For live meetings, check how a tool captures the conversation and whether that method is acceptable to participants. For recordings you already own, start with file import. For your final deliverable, decide whether you need a transcript, a decision log or a list of assigned tasks. These are different outputs: a readable summary cannot replace a searchable record when you need to check what was said.</p>
<h2>A capture-to-handoff checklist</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What to check</th><th>A useful acceptance check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Capture</td><td>Meeting platform, recording permissions, file types and languages</td><td>Your permitted sample reaches the transcription step</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review</td><td>Timestamps, speaker correction and playback</td><td>You can locate and correct an uncertain sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output</td><td>Transcript export, summary export and sharing settings</td><td>The result opens in the place your team actually works</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ownership</td><td>Who can see, download or delete the recording</td><td>You can explain access to every participant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Billing</td><td>Per-recording limits, included usage, seats and renewal</td><td>Your expected recording fits the selected plan</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Use a short, permitted sample</h2>
<p>Before paying, try a recording you are allowed to process that includes normal background noise, a name or acronym, a proposed deadline and a sentence where someone changes their mind. Keep a reference transcript of those passages. Compare the output with the audio rather than another AI answer. This is our suggested evaluation method, not a completed product test or accuracy benchmark.</p>
<p>Write down corrections you needed, whether speakers were assigned correctly, and whether a deadline was a suggestion or a commitment. Try the export as well. If the tool produces notes that must be copied and reformatted manually, include that work in your decision.</p>
<h2>Read more than the monthly allowance</h2>
<p>A monthly transcription allowance does not tell you the maximum usable length of one conversation. Check the individual recording limit, import allowance, summary allowance and whether exports are available on the plan you are considering. Confirm whether quoted prices require annual payment, apply per seat or exclude tax. Look at the renewal and cancellation terms before entering payment details.</p>
<h2>An official-source example</h2>
<p>Notta describes live meeting capture, uploaded audio/video transcription and AI summaries. Its pricing page lists a Free plan with both a monthly transcription allowance and a per-conversation limit. Those separate constraints are a useful reminder to inspect a full plan table rather than the largest number in an advertisement. We have not run a hands-on comparison or verified a promotional coupon.</p>
<p>Check the <a href="https://www.notta.ai/en">official product description</a> and <a href="https://www.notta.ai/en/pricing">official plan comparison</a>. These are direct source links, not affiliate links. This example does not establish that it is the best option for your meetings.</p>
<h2>When to postpone a purchase</h2>
<p>Postpone if you cannot export the output you need, cannot correct important errors, or do not know who can access the recording. A tool with fewer advertised features can be a better fit if the handoff is easier to verify. Follow our <a href="/ai-tools/verify-ai-meeting-summaries/">summary verification checklist</a> before sharing generated notes.</p>'''),
    ('audio-video-transcription', 'Audio and video transcription: a practical workflow',
     'Prepare permitted recordings, correct transcripts and export usable notes without treating AI output as a verified record.',
     '''<h2>Start with permission and an intact source</h2>
<p>For your own tutorial, interview or meeting recording, preserve the original and work on a copy. For another person's video, check the owner's terms and permission before downloading, transcribing or republishing it. A publicly watchable video is not permission to publish its transcript. This guide is workflow advice, not a determination of recording or copyright law.</p>
<h2>Define what you actually need</h2>
<p>A transcript preserves words, a subtitle file adds timed segments, and a summary selects conclusions. Decide which output the task requires. If you need subtitles, verify that the chosen export includes timestamps and opens correctly in your player or editor. If you need research notes, preserve source timestamps so that a claim can be checked without replaying the entire recording.</p>
<h2>Prepare a useful sample</h2>
<p>Listen to the source first. Note where sound drops out, where speakers overlap and where technical terms occur. Do not assume an enhancement filter recovers words that were never captured. Test a copy before processing the complete recording. Avoid repeatedly converting files merely to satisfy an upload limit; check accepted formats, file size and recording duration first.</p>
<h2>Review the high-consequence passages</h2>
<p>Correct names, amounts, dates, negations and instructions against the audio. A missing word such as “not” can reverse the meaning of a sentence. Keep uncertain passages marked as uncertain instead of completing them from context. For an interview, verify speaker labels before attributing a quotation. For a technical tutorial, check command names and file paths separately; a fluent transcript can still contain an unusable command.</p>
<h2>Keep transcription and translation separate</h2>
<p>When the output language differs from the recording, retain the source transcript as well as the translation. Resolve uncertain source words before translating them. Do not use a polished translation as evidence that the original transcription was correct. If a language or mixed-language feature is important, check the provider's current support documentation and test your own permitted sample.</p>
<h2>Export before you commit</h2>
<p>Open the downloaded file in its destination application. Check encoding, punctuation, timestamps and paragraph breaks. For a subtitle file, play it alongside the video and check whether the timings remain synchronized. Keep an unedited export and a corrected copy so later changes can be compared. Share only the version you intend recipients to use.</p>
<h2>A reusable handoff record</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>What to write</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Source</td><td>Recording title and a permitted source location</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output</td><td>Transcript, subtitles, translation or summary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review</td><td>Who checked names, dates and uncertain passages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unresolved</td><td>Passages that still need confirmation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access</td><td>Who may read the file and when it should be removed</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For buying checks, see <a href="/ai-tools/ai-meeting-notes/">our meeting-tool guide</a>. For a product example, the <a href="https://www.notta.ai/en">official Notta feature page</a> describes transcription of uploaded files. Verify the current plan and export requirements on its <a href="https://www.notta.ai/en/pricing">pricing page</a>. We do not claim this workflow was tested in that product.</p>'''),
    ('verify-ai-meeting-summaries', 'Verify AI meeting summaries before sharing them',
     'Check decisions, owners, deadlines and recording access before an AI summary becomes a team record.',
     '''<h2>Verify commitments, not just grammar</h2>
<p>Before sharing an AI meeting summary, compare each decision, task owner and deadline with the recording or a participant's confirmation. An attractive format does not make the content authoritative. Keep unresolved questions visible rather than turning them into decisions for the sake of a tidy recap.</p>
<h2>Separate suggestions from decisions</h2>
<p>Consider an illustrative meeting where someone says “Could we launch on Friday?” and another person says “We need the security review first.” A summary saying “The team agreed to launch Friday” would discard the condition. This is a fictional example, not a transcript from a product test. A better record labels Friday as proposed and the security review as a prerequisite until participants confirm a commitment.</p>
<h2>Build an evidence-linked decision log</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Review question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Decision</td><td>Was it accepted, proposed or still disputed?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner</td><td>Did this person accept the task?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deadline</td><td>Is the date explicit, and are timezone or prerequisites relevant?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Can a reviewer locate the supporting passage?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Open issue</td><td>What still needs confirmation?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If you cannot find supporting evidence for an action item, mark it for confirmation instead of sending it as an instruction. Check an important decision with its owner when the audio is ambiguous. AI output should not create an obligation that participants did not accept.</p>
<h2>Check recording access before the meeting</h2>
<p>Agree on whether the meeting may be recorded and processed with the selected service. Review your organization's policy, the provider's retention and deletion controls, and who can access transcripts or shared links. Use a nonsensitive sample for evaluation. Do not upload customer secrets, credentials or identity documents merely to test a note-taking tool.</p>
<p>Confirm the scope of a shared link before distributing it. A summary intended for colleagues may contain details that should not appear in a public link. Share the minimum necessary version, and establish who can remove it. If a workflow cannot meet your confidentiality needs, use an approved alternative rather than relying on a general marketing security claim.</p>
<h2>Make corrections traceable</h2>
<p>Keep the original output and a corrected version with a reviewer and review date. If a correction changes a decision already sent to recipients, issue an explicit correction rather than silently replacing the file. Preserve only what your retention policy permits; keeping every recording forever creates its own access burden.</p>
<h2>Evaluate the full handoff</h2>
<p>Test the path from recording to the final team document. Ask whether a recipient can distinguish confirmed decisions from open issues without asking you to explain the summary. Choose a tool only after checking that workflow. Our <a href="/ai-tools/ai-meeting-notes/">buying checklist</a> and <a href="/ai-tools/audio-video-transcription/">transcription guide</a> cover the preceding steps. These recommendations are editorial judgment, not measured productivity or accuracy claims.</p>'''),
]


def publish_meeting_pages(page, date):
    for slug, title, description, content in GUIDES:
        if slug == 'audio-video-transcription':
            content = content + WW_AD_VIDEO
            affiliate_note = ('This page contains a labelled affiliate advertisement. '
                              'No verified promotional coupon is claimed.')
        else:
            affiliate_note = ('No affiliate commission is configured '
                              'and no verified promotional coupon is claimed.')
        body = ('<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">AI WORKFLOW GUIDE</p>'
                f'<h1>{title}</h1><p class="lede">{description}</p></section>'
                '<article class="prose">' + content +
                '<p>Independent editorial guidance. ' + affiliate_note + ' '
                'No hands-on product benchmark is claimed.</p>'
                '<p><a href="/ai-tools/">← All AI tools and practical guides</a></p></article>')
        page('/ai-tools/' + slug + '/', title, description, body, lastmod=date)
